  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough next Sunday...\n\nPrecipitation continues over much of the region as of noon but\nstarting to see breaks across the west (downslope effects). We \nremain under the diffluent region of the upper level jet however \nso this event isn't finished. Over the next 12-18hrs the trof axis\nwill slowly migrate northward and precip will gradually taper \noff. Look for 0.20-0.60\" of additional rainfall from Treasure and \nRosebud Counties eastward, and less to the west. Regarding \nconvection this afternoon/evening, non-severe t-storms are \npossible in our far east and over southern mountains & foothills, \nthe latter of which will be under the PV axis and cooler temps \naloft by evening, and aided by a weak secondary shortwave lifting \nout of southeast ID, on southern flank of main upper low. Snow \nlevel at or above 10kft should fall to near 9.5kft by evening, \nthus some convective snow showers are expected over the high \nelevations of the Beartooths. Will let Winter Wx Advisory for \nwintry travel impacts over the Beartooth Highway continue, but\naccums will be minimal.\n\nAs precip dissipates and shifts northward tonight, the downslope\nwinds should prevent a risk of fog despite the recent rainfall,\nwhich has exceeded 2\" in some areas. \n\nSatellite imagery shows another trailing wave over OR. This\nfeature will provide some ascent which along with diurnal heating\nwill result in scattered afternoon/evening showers & t-storms on\nMonday. For the most part this activity will be non-severe, but\nthere are signs that if a southeasterly low level jet develops by\nevening the increased shear & instability could support a couple\nstronger cells in southeast MT (mainly in Powder River & Carter \nCounties). Something to monitor.\n\nUpper low remains over southern SK Tue/Wed, keeping us under\ncyclonic flow, thus at least a low (20-30%) chance of showers and\nt-storms each day. There may be a brief risk of severe storms in\nthe far east on Tuesday, but all indications are that the much \nmore favorable environment will reside in the Dakotas. Otherwise, \nthe air mass is looking more stable Wednesday so this will be the \nmore benign day.\n\nWeather pattern remains active and another Pacific wave arrives \nThursday. As temps warm gradually the next few days (low-mid 70s\nMon-Wed, 75-80F Thu) and winds shift to the E-SE, it could be a \nday to watch for potential severe t-storms. This is certainly a \nsignal among AI convective models and ensembles show a distinct\ninstability maximum on this day. If it materializes, there would\nbe a risk of hail on Thursday.\n\nBy the end of the work week, flow backs to the SW ahead of the \nnext deepening Pacific coast trof. Friday is looking mostly dry\nand it will be warmer (upper 70s to mid 80s). Temps should push \n90F by Saturday. At some point over the weekend we will see \nconvective potential increase with energy emerging from the \nPacific trof, and this could also be a time to watch for severe \npotential. It would appear that we are entering our more active \nthunderstorm season. Recent precip here and across the high plains\nwill help keep boundary layer moisture elevated, and promote \nthunderstorm activity.\n\nJKL",
